# Mosquitto ACL - Hardening Guide s5 [Recommended]
#
#   per-gateway credentials, write-only to its own omp/{site}/... subtree;
#   platform consumers read-only.
#
# The asymmetry is the point. A compromised gateway can fabricate its own
# machines' history - nothing removes that - but it must not be able to
# forge another site's, or to read the floor back out. A compromised
# consumer must not be able to write anything at all.
#
# Install:
#   listener 8883
#   acl_file /etc/mosquitto/omp.acl
#   password_file /etc/mosquitto/omp.passwd
#   allow_anonymous false
# then: sudo mosquitto_passwd -c /etc/mosquitto/omp.passwd gw-dhaka-01

# Nobody gets anything by default.
topic

# --- gateways: write only, own subtree only -----------------------------
# One block per gateway. The username SHOULD be the gateway_id, so the
# broker's auth log and the envelopes' gateway_id field agree during an
# incident (s8 step 4).
user gw-dhaka-01
topic write omp/dhaka/+/+/+/+

user gw-dhaka-02
topic write omp/dhaka/+/+/+/+

# --- consumers: read only ------------------------------------------------
user platform-ingest
topic read omp/#

# A per-site consumer, e.g. a brand's auditor given one site's data only.
user auditor-dhaka
topic read omp/dhaka/#

# --- deliberately absent -------------------------------------------------
# No user has write access to another site's subtree, and no gateway can
# read. There is no command topic in OMP at all: the protocol is read-only
# at the edge, and the broker should not be the place that quietly changes
# that.
